      "questionText": "Explain the four core principles of the System of Rice Intensification (SRI) developed in Madagascar.",
      "modelAnswer": "The System of Rice Intensification (SRI), developed in 1983 by Fr. Henri de Laulani\u00e9 in Madagascar, is an agro-ecological methodology that modifies plant, soil, water, and nutrient management to maximize individual plant potential:\n\n1. Transplanting Very Young Single Seedlings:\n- Traditional transplanting uses 25-35 day-old seedlings in clumps of 3-4 plants.\n- SRI transplants a single, delicate seedling per hill at 8 to 12 days old (at the 2-leaf stage) with the seed sac attached, preserving root apical meristems and capitalizing on early phyllochronic tillering.\n\n2. Wide Square Planting Geometry:\n- Seedlings are planted shallowly (1-2 cm deep) in a wide square grid of 25 cm \u00d7 25 cm (or 30 cm \u00d7 30 cm).\n- This wide geometry eliminates plant-to-plant competition, allowing prolific tillering (30-50 productive tillers/hill) and expansive root development.\n\n3. Aerobic Soil Moisture Management (Alternate Wetting and Drying - AWD):\n- Continuous deep flooding is completely abolished. Fields are kept moist but unflooded during the vegetative phase.\n- Water is applied intermittently only when hair-line cracks appear on the soil surface, oxygenating the root zone and preventing root asphyxiation.\n\n4. Mechanical Soil Aeration and Weed Incorporation (Cono-Weeder):\n- Operating a motorized or manual Cono-weeder 3 to 4 times between square rows churls weeds into the mud as green manure while aerating the rhizosphere, stimulating root microbial symbiosis.",
