      "questionText": "Describe the symptoms, etiology, and remedial agronomic management of Khaira disease of rice.",
      "modelAnswer": "Khaira disease is a widespread physiological nutritional disorder in rice soils:\n\n1. Etiology and Discovery:\n- First identified in 1966 by Dr. Y. L. Nene at G.B. Pant University of Agriculture and Technology, Pantnagar, Uttarakhand.\n- Caused by the physiological deficiency of available Zinc ($Zn^{2+}$) in soil. Highly prevalent in calcareous soils, alkaline sodic soils ($pH > 7.8$), and intensive rice-wheat rotation belts where continuous flooding and high phosphorus applications fix zinc into insoluble compounds.\n\n2. Diagnostic Visual Symptoms:\n- Appears 2 to 3 weeks after transplanting in nursery or field.\n- The youngest leaves emerge with mild interveinal chlorosis, but the characteristic symptom appears on middle leaves as small, irregular reddish-brown to bronze pigmentation spots.\n- These bronze spots coalesce rapidly, turning the entire leaf blade rusty-brown; leaf tissue becomes brittle and withers.\n- Plants suffer severe stunting, root elongation ceases, and roots turn dark brown; tillering is severely arrested, resulting in uneven, patchy crop stands.\n\n3. Remedial Agronomic Management:\n- Prophylactic Soil Application: Apply Zinc Sulphate ($ZnSO_4 \\cdot 7H_2O$) @ 25 kg/ha as a basal broadcast during final puddling (sufficient for 2-3 successive crop seasons).\n- Standing Crop Curative Treatment: If symptoms appear in the field, spray a foliar solution:\n  $$5.0 \\text{ kg } ZnSO_4 + 2.5 \\text{ kg Slaked Lime [} Ca(OH)_2 \\text{]} \\text{ dissolved in 500 liters of water per hectare}$$\n  *(Lime is added to neutralize acidity and prevent foliar chemical scorching).*\n  Repeat the spray after 10-12 days if recovery is slow.",
