      "questionText": "Outline Sturtevant's classification of maize into seven commercial types based on kernel endosperm characteristics.",
      "modelAnswer": "In 1899, E. Lewis Sturtevant classified maize (*Zea mays* L.) into seven distinct botanical groups based on kernel structural composition and endosperm starch properties:\n\n1. *Zea mays indentata* (Dent Corn):\n- Kernel endosperm contains soft floury starch in the core and hard flint starch on the outer sides. Differential drying causes the crown to collapse inward, forming a characteristic 'dent'. Dominant in commercial grain production.\n\n2. *Zea mays indurata* (Flint Corn):\n- Endosperm consists of a thick, hard, corneous starch layer enclosing a small soft core. Kernels are hard, smooth, and rounded. Extensively cultivated in India due to insect storage resistance.\n\n3. *Zea mays saccharata* (Sweet Corn):\n- Possesses recessive genes that prevent the conversion of soluble sugar into starch. Kernels are sweet, translucent, and shriveled when dry; harvested green at 70% moisture for table use.\n\n4. *Zea mays everta* (Popcorn):\n- Extreme variant of flint corn with very small kernels containing dense corneous endosperm. When heated to 170-180\u00b0C, internal moisture vaporizes into steam, exploding the pericarp inside out.\n\n5. *Zea mays amylacea* (Flour Corn):\n- Endosperm is composed entirely of soft, floury starch with zero flint layers. Kernels are soft, easily masticated and milled into flour.\n\n6. *Zea mays tunicata* (Pod Corn):\n- A primitive, ornamental ancestral type where every individual kernel is enclosed in its own floral bract (glume/husk).\n\n7. *Zea mays ceratina* (Waxy Corn):\n- Endosperm starch contains nearly 100% branched amylopectin and zero linear amylose, giving a waxy appearance; used in textile and industrial adhesives.",
