      "questionText": "What is Quality Protein Maize (QPM)? Detail the genetic modification involved and its nutritional significance over traditional maize.",
      "modelAnswer": "Quality Protein Maize (QPM) represents an outstanding biofortification achievement developed by Dr. Surinder Vasal and Dr. Evangelina Villegas at CIMMYT, Mexico (awarded the World Food Prize in 2000):\n\n1. Genetic Modification Architecture:\n- Traditional maize protein (Zein) is nutritionally defective, containing deficient levels of two essential amino acids: **Lysine** and **Tryptophan**.\n- QPM incorporates the recessive mutant gene **opaque-2 (*o2*)** (and *floury-2*). The *opaque-2* mutation suppresses the synthesis of zein proteins and upregulates the synthesis of non-zein fractions (albumins, globulins, and glutelins) rich in lysine and tryptophan.\n- Because original *o2* mutants had soft, chalky endosperms vulnerable to grain weevils and ear rots, CIMMYT breeders introduced genetic endosperm modifiers that restored the hard, vitreous, flint texture without sacrificing amino acid gains.\n\n2. Nutritional Significance:\n- Doubled Essential Amino Acids: QPM contains 100% higher Lysine (from 1.6% up to 4.0% of total protein) and Tryptophan (from 0.4% up to 1.0%), doubling biological protein value.\n- High Biological Value: Protein quality in QPM achieves 90% of the biological value of skimmed cow's milk protein.\n- Alleviating Malnutrition: Replaces low-protein cereals in tribal and rural diets, combating Kwashiorkor and childhood stunting.\n- Commercial Poultry and Swine Feed: Elevates feed conversion efficiency in broiler poultry, drastically reducing the need for expensive synthetic lysine supplements.\n\nProminent Indian QPM Hybrids: HQPM-1, HQPM-5, HQPM-7, Shaktiman-1, Shaktiman-2, and Shaktiman-4.",
