      "questionText": "Explain the biochemical basis of Dhurrin/HCN toxicity in Sorghum. What safety precautions must a dairy farmer observe when feeding sorghum fodder?",
      "modelAnswer": "Hydrocyanic Acid (HCN), or Prussic acid, toxicity is a fatal hazard associated with feeding immature or drought-stressed sorghum forage:\n\n1. Biochemical Mechanism of Toxicity:\n- Young sorghum vegetative tissues contain the cyanogenic glycoside **Dhurrin** located in the vacuoles of epidermal leaf cells.\n- When plant tissue is crushed (by mastication in the bovine mouth or chopping), dhurrin comes into contact with the enzyme **dhurrin $\\beta$-glucosidase** in mesophyll cells, releasing glucose and $p$-hydroxybenzaldehyde cyanohydrin.\n- Hydroxynitrile lyase subsequently dissociates this compound, releasing free gaseous **Hydrocyanic Acid (HCN)**.\n- In the bovine rumen, absorbed cyanide ($CN^-$) binds strongly to ferric iron ($Fe^{3+}$) in cytochrome oxidase enzymes within cell mitochondria, blocking cellular respiration and causing acute tissue asphyxiation (anoxia). Animals collapse within 15-30 minutes with bright red venous blood.\n\n2. Critical Safety Precautions for Dairy Farmers:\n- Stage of Harvest: Never harvest sorghum for green fodder before 50% flowering (or before plants reach at least 50-60 cm height). At flowering, HCN concentrations drop below safe limits (<200 ppm).\n- Avoid Drought-Stressed Regrowth: Never feed stunted, moisture-stressed ratoon regrowth or second-flush shoots following prolonged dry spells, where dhurrin concentration spikes to lethal levels (>1,000 ppm).\n- Hay and Silage Curing: Curing sorghum into dry hay or ensiling it for 4-6 weeks dissipates 80-90% of volatile HCN gas, making it completely safe for livestock.\n- Antidote Administration: If accidental ingestion occurs, immediately inject intravenous Sodium Nitrite (3%) followed by Sodium Thiosulfate (25%).",
