      "questionText": "Describe the floral biology of Pearl Millet (*Pennisetum glaucum*). How does Protogyny facilitate commercial hybrid seed production?",
      "modelAnswer": "Pearl Millet (Bajra) possesses a specialized floral architecture adapted for obligate cross-pollination:\n\n1. Floral Biology and Protogyny:\n- The inflorescence of pearl millet is a dense, cylindrical spike (panicle) bearing thousands of paired spikelets.\n- Pearl millet is strictly **Protogynous**: the feathery female stigmas emerge and become receptive 2 to 4 days before the male anthers on the same panicle begin shedding pollen.\n- Stigmas emerge from the middle of the panicle and proceed upward and downward, remaining receptive for 2-3 days. By the time anthers dehisce on that panicle, its own stigmas have already withered or been pollinated by foreign wind-borne pollen.\n- This chronological separation of sex organ maturity prevents self-pollination, enforcing virtually 100% natural cross-pollination (anemophily / wind pollination).\n\n2. Facilitation of Hybrid Seed Production:\n- Natural Emasculation: Because female stigmas emerge cleanly before pollen shedding, manual emasculation (which is impossible on thousands of microscopic florets) is unnecessary.\n- Cytoplasmic Male Sterility (CMS) Utilization: Commercial hybrids utilize a Cytoplasmic Genetic Male Sterility (CGMS) system (A-line, B-line, R-line) developed from the Tift 23A cytoplasm:\n  - Female seed-parent rows (A-line) lack viable pollen.\n  - Male pollinator rows (R-line) are planted in alternating ratios (4A : 2R).\n  - Wind effortlessly blows pollen from R-line anthers onto the receptive, protogynous stigmas of the A-line, ensuring high seed set of $F_1$ hybrid seed without requiring costly hand pollination.",
