      "questionText": "Explain the operational methodology of Alternate Wetting and Drying (AWD) in rice. What are its environmental benefits regarding methane reduction?",
      "modelAnswer": "Alternate Wetting and Drying (AWD) is a climate-smart water management technology developed by the International Rice Research Institute (IRRI) that disproves the myth that rice requires continuous submergence:\n\n1. Operational Methodology (The 'Pani Pipe' Protocol):\n- A 30-cm long perforated plastic (PVC) pipe (10-15 cm diameter) is inserted 15 cm into the puddled paddy soil, leaving 15 cm protruding above the ground. Soil is scooped out from inside the pipe so the water level inside matches the field water table.\n- Initial Submergence: The field is kept shallowly flooded (2-3 cm) for the first 2 weeks after transplanting to facilitate seedling recovery and weed suppression.\n- The AWD Cycle: Thereafter, the field is allowed to dry out naturally. As the water table recedes, the farmer inspects the water depth inside the pipe.\n- Irrigation Trigger: When the water level inside the pipe drops to **15 cm below the soil surface**, irrigation is applied to re-flood the field to a depth of 5 cm.\n- Sensitive Window Exemption: Continuous 5-cm flooding is maintained during the critical heading/flowering window (1 week before to 1 week after flowering) to avoid spikelet sterility.\n\n2. Environmental and Economic Benefits:\n- 25-30% Water Savings: Reduces field irrigation requirements by up to 30% without any reduction in grain yield.\n- 30-50% Reduction in Methane ($CH_4$) Emissions: Flooded soils foster anaerobic methanogenic archaea that digest organic matter into potent methane gas. Periodic aerobic drying oxygenates the topsoil, halting methanogenesis and stimulating methanotrophic bacteria that consume methane.\n- Enhanced Root Anchorage and Lodging Resistance: Aerobic drying promotes deeper, thicker root growth, reducing plant lodging during harvest.",
