      "id": "AGP218-U2-SUB17",
      "questionType": "COMPARISON",
      "maxMarks": 6,
      "difficulty": "Hard",
      "syllabusTopic": "Rice cultivation methods: Direct Seeded Rice (DSR), Transplanting, and System of Rice Intensification (SRI)",
      "questionText": "Compare Conventional Puddled Transplanted Rice (TPR) and Direct Seeded Rice (DSR) on root morphology, soil physical properties, greenhouse gas emissions, and economic profitability.",
      "modelAnswer": "The shift from TPR to DSR represents a profound agro-ecological and physical transformation:\n\n1. Root Morphology and Distribution:\n- Transplanted Rice (TPR): Puddled mud creates a shallow, compacted plow-pan at 10-15 cm depth. Roots are predominantly adventitious, shallow, horizontally dispersed, and confined to the upper 10 cm mud layer, possessing thick aerenchyma for flooded survival.\n- Direct Seeded Rice (DSR): Soil is unpuddled and well-aerated. Rice develops a deeper, thicker, vertically penetrating seminal and crown root system (penetrating down to 25-35 cm), with greater root biomass density, conferring superior lodging resistance and drought buffering.\n\n2. Impact on Soil Physical Properties:\n- Transplanted Rice (TPR): Puddling deliberately destroys soil aggregates, disperses clay particles, and compacts pore spaces to form an impervious hardpan to prevent water percolation. This destroys soil structure, resulting in poor aeration, soil crusting, and reduced root penetration for succeeding upland crops (e.g. wheat in the Rice-Wheat system).\n- Direct Seeded Rice (DSR): Preserves soil structure, macro-porosity, and aggregate stability. Infiltration rates remain intact, enhancing root aeration, microbial diversity, and boosting the yield of succeeding winter wheat/chickpea crops.\n\n3. Greenhouse Gas (GHG) Emission Dynamics:\n- Transplanted Rice (TPR): Anaerobic, submerged soil ($E_h < -150 \\text{ mV}$) drives massive methane ($CH_4$) emissions from methanogenic archaea; accounts for ~10% of global agricultural GHG emissions.\n- Direct Seeded Rice (DSR): Aerobic soil conditions virtually eliminate methane emissions (slashing $CH_4$ by 70-85%). However, alternate wetting cycles slightly elevate nitrous oxide ($N_2O$) emissions, though total Global Warming Potential (GWP) is reduced by 30-40%.\n\n4. Economic Cost and Profitability:\n- Transplanted Rice (TPR): High production costs due to nursery raising, puddling fuel costs (2-3 tractor passes), and scarce manual transplanting labor (30-40 person-days/ha).\n- Direct Seeded Rice (DSR): Saves \u20b96,000 to \u20b99,000 per hectare in nursery, labor, and diesel costs, delivering higher net benefit-cost ratios despite higher herbicide expenditures.",
      "outlinePoints": [
        "Root architecture: Shallow horizontal root mat on plow-pan vs Deep vertical root penetration (30 cm)",
        "Soil physics: Destruction of macro-pores via puddling vs Preservation of aggregate structure",
        "Impact on succeeding crops: Soil compaction hurting wheat vs Healthy structure aiding rabi crops",
        "GHG emissions: Severe methane emissions in TPR vs 75% methane reduction in aerobic DSR",
        "Economic profitability: High nursery/labor costs vs \u20b98,000/ha operational savings in DSR"
      ]
